from register_common import add

add(1,'Universal incidence and base change',[4,5,6,7],143,280,'proved there','B',
'For a finite locally free E of rank r+1, r≥1, a quotient E^vee→L defines H=P(E/L^-1)⊂P(E). Universal incidence is a projective bundle over each factor, and pullback to any X→P(E), including a pulled-back quasi-coherent module, commutes with base change.',
'The quotient convention and incidence construction agree with Stacks01OA and the universal-divisor construction in Stacks0FD4. This is established projective-bundle geometry; no new generality is inferred from the mistyped target of G on scan7.',
'Stacks01OA,Stacks0FD4','Proof: locally choose a basis with the quotient coordinate a unit; the one linear equation eliminates that coordinate and gives a polynomial chart. Base change preserves that presentation.',group='incidence')
add(2,'Generic irreducibility and dominance',[8],302,308,'proved there','B',
'For an irreducible finite-type k-scheme X and any k-morphism X→P^r, its section over the generic point of the dual space is empty or irreducible; if nonempty it dominates X. This is ordinary, not geometric, irreducibility.',
'The incidence projective-bundle argument proves this exact statement. Stacks0G4C separately treats the stronger geometric version, whose image-dimension hypothesis must not be omitted.',
'Stacks0G4C','Proof: the total incidence over X is irreducible. Localizing its coordinate rings at the nonzero functions of the dual base gives an empty scheme or an irreducible generic fiber, whose generic point is also generic in the incidence and hence maps to the generic point of X.',group='generic')
add(3,'Which subsets the generic hyperplane sees',[8,9],309,341,'proved there','B',
'The image in P^r of the generic hyperplane consists exactly of the nonclosed points. For constructible Z⊂X of finite type over k, its generic section is empty iff f(Z) is finite.',
'The exact visibility statement follows from projective incidence and the fact that a positive-dimensional projective closed subset meets every hyperplane. Stacks0G4C uses the same generic-section setup.',
'Stacks0G4C','Proof: a closed point has finite residue extension, so its incidence image is a proper hyperplane locus. The closure of a nonclosed point has positive dimension; if a hyperplane missed it, that closure would be both affine and proper, hence finite. A constructible subset of a finite-type Jacobson space consisting only of closed points is finite.',group='generic')
add(4,'Equality in Corollary2.4 needs both irreducibilities',[9],342,350,'false as stated','B',
'The live equality clause says that, for closed Z,Z′ with Z irreducible and dim f(Z)>0, equality of their generic sections implies Z=Z′; the irreducibility of Z′ was deleted.',
'The implication fails by an invisible isolated component. The inclusion implication remains valid; the symmetric equality implication holds when both closed subsets are irreducible and have nonempty generic sections.',
'Stacks0G4C','Proof: take X=P², Z a line and Z′=Z∪{p} with p outside that line. All hypotheses on Z hold. The generic hyperplane misses p, so Z_eta=Z′_eta, although Z≠Z′.',
refine='If Z and Z′ are both irreducible closed subsets with positive-dimensional images, equality of their generic sections implies Z=Z′, by applying dominance to both inclusions.',group='generic')
add(5,'Components and dimension of generic sections',[9,10],352,403,'proof sketched','B',
'Surviving irreducible components and associated-prime supports correspond under generic incidence. For finite-type X, if every irreducible component has positive-dimensional image, dim X_eta=dim X−1; componentwise versions use the corresponding component dimensions. Associated-prime assertions require the quasi-coherent/coherent and local noetherian hypotheses used on the page.',
'Projective-bundle flatness with geometrically integral fibers and localization establish the correspondences. The surviving insertion excluding contracted components is essential. Standard associated-prime base-change results in EGAIV2§3 supply the sheaf version.',
'EGAIV2,Stacks0G4C','Proof: incidence over each component has relative dimension r−1; its dominant map to the r-dimensional dual base lowers dimension by r. Non-dominant components disappear. Associated primes are computed after a polynomial extension and localization.',group='local_properties')
add(6,'Codepth and Serre conditions under the generic section',[11,12],415,476,'proof sketched','A',
'For coherent F on a finite-type k-scheme, the surviving generic-section stalks have the same codepth as the corresponding F-stalks under the regular local incidence map. CM, S_k, regularity, R_k and normality consequently compare with the indicated finite-image bad loci; geometric variants require the later hypotheses of2.12–2.18.',
'The commutative-algebra mechanisms are in published EGAIV2§§5–6. Constructibility is a separate EGAIV3§12 input; the page’s request for those references is not a new unresolved Bertini assertion.',
'EGAIV2,EGAIV3','Proof: the local map is a localization of a polynomial extension with regular fiber. The flat-local depth and dimension formulas add the same fiber dimension, so codepth is unchanged. Serre conditions are then tested on corresponding generizations.',group='local_properties')
add(7,'Smoothness and geometric local properties',[12,13,14,15],478,607,'proof sketched','B',
'If f:X→P^r is unramified and X is of finite type over k, geometric smoothness, R_k, reducedness and normality compare at x and its surviving generic-section point y as in2.12–2.18; without unramifiedness these comparisons are retained in characteristic0, not in arbitrary characteristic.',
'Stacks0FD4 proves the immersion smooth-Bertini statement by the exact first-jet codimension argument. Étale localization of an unramified map gives the indicated extension. The page itself warns about purely inseparable covers; the elementary auxiliary lemma in its proof is separately false.',
'Stacks0FD4,EGAIV4','Proof: in the smooth case the evaluation map onto O_x/m_x² has rank dim_xX+1. Hyperplanes singular at x have that codimension in the space of sections; incidence dimension excludes a dominant bad family. Étale-local immersions preserve these rank calculations.',group='smooth_bertini')
add(8,'The nowhere-zero generic-combination lemma',[12,13],507,514,'false as stated','B',
'The proof claims that a generic linear combination of global generating sections of a nonzero vector bundle is nowhere zero on the whole scalar-extended scheme.',
'This is false even for a line bundle on A¹. The valid Bertini proof tests the section and its differential together on the incidence locus, as in Stacks0FD4, rather than asserting this global nowhere-vanishing property.',
'Stacks0FD4','computations.py generic_combination_zero: E=O_A1 generated by1,t; over K=k(S1,S2), S1+S2t vanishes at t=−S1/S2. E is locally free of positive rank and the generators really generate at every point.',
refine='For a smooth X and an immersion defined by a generating linear system, the locus of sections whose zero scheme is singular at a fixed geometric point has codimension dim_xX+1; its incidence has dimension less than the parameter space.',group='smooth_bertini')
add(9,'Bertini–Zariski irreducibility and the field lemma',[15,16,17],609,692,'proof sketched','E',
'For a geometrically irreducible finite-type X/k and f:X→P^r with dim f(X)≥2, the generic hyperplane section is geometrically irreducible. The function-field lemma says K(S_i) is primary over k(S_i,a0+ΣaiSi) if K/k is primary and trdeg_k k(a0,…,am)≥2. Geometric integrality additionally uses unramified f or characteristic0 as written.',
'Samuel’s1951 Bourbaki exposition of Seidenberg gives the classical geometric-irreducibility argument in characteristic0 and states the positive-characteristic extension. Stacks0G4C Lemma37.32.1 supplies the exact primary-field proof in arbitrary characteristic; its finite-field reduction is explicit.',
'Samuel1952,Stacks0G4C','Targeted proof comparison: the finite collection of intermediate fields and translation argument in the opened Stacks lemma matches the page’s requested field-theoretic step; no independent historical priority beyond the cited1950/1951 sources is claimed.',group='generic')
add(10,'Connected generic sections',[17,18],693,735,'proof sketched','E',
'Over an algebraically closed field, assume each irreducible component of X has image dimension≥2 and deleting closed subsets with finite image does not disconnect X. Then the generic hyperplane section is connected. The geometric formulation is obtained after algebraic closure.',
'This follows from classical Bertini irreducibility on each component and a chain of component intersections surviving the generic section. Stacks0G4C supplies the geometric-irreducibility input; this exact connectivity hypothesis is stronger than bare connectedness.',
'Samuel1952,Stacks0G4C','Proof: a disconnection of the generic section partitions surviving components. Their pairwise intersections across the partition have finite image, so their finite union gives a forbidden deletion disconnecting X.',group='connectedness')
add(11,'Constructibility and specialization of good hyperplanes',[18,19,20],738,830,'proof sketched','A',
'For finitely presented families and finitely presented flat sheaves, the listed fiber properties give constructible good-parameter sets; a generic good fiber therefore gives a dense open. Openness of global good loci additionally uses the stated proper-support and flatness hypotheses.',
'Published EGA IV3 9.7.7 (printed79) gives local constructibility of geometric irreducibility, connectedness, reducedness and integrality for finitely presented morphisms. For proper finitely presented f and base-flat finitely presented F,12.2.1 (printed179–181) gives the listed open sheaf-property loci, including equidimensionality where required. For proper flat finitely presented schemes,12.2.4 (printed183) gives the exact S_k, geometric R_k with equidimensional/no-embedded-component assumptions, smooth, normal, reduced, and integral variants. Benoist2011 Proposition2.1 gives the related proper-flat equidimensional geometric-irreducibility/generic-reducedness variant. These do not make every listed property open without its hypotheses.',
'EGAIV3,Benoist2011',group='spreading')
add(12,'C_m connectivity under a hyperplane section',[20,21],831,866,'corrected by himself later','C',
'The proposed property C_m means no disconnection after deleting a closed subset of dimension≤m. The generic drop C_(m+1)⇒C_m is corrected on scan21 to require quasi-finite f, or to use image dimensions in place of dimensions on X. Constructibility of the generalized property is requested, not proved here.',
'Classical connectedness and EGAIV3 constructibility settle the component-chain ingredients. Searches of Bertini connectedness, Jouanolou, EGA and SGA did not identify the exact all-m relative C_m formulation with the draft’s quantifiers. The residual is that precise formulation and proof, not ordinary Bertini connectedness.',
'EGAIV3,Stacks0G4C',confidence='medium',group='connectedness')
add(13,'Relative regular sections and their good locus',[21,22,23,24],868,1032,'corrected by himself later','A',
'For an S-flat finitely presented module F and a line-bundle section on X, fiberwise F-regularity is open on the zero scheme; the good hyperplane locus is constructible and fiberwise dense. With support F proper/S it is open. Regular quotients preserve CM and lower the general S_k index by one.',
'The text cancelled at4.5 is expressly reinstated at5.1 as cancelled by mistake. Published EGA IV3 11.3.7–8 (printed135–136), and Stacks056P Lemmas31.19.1–9, supply the relative regular-section/flatness statements. EGA IV3 12.2.1–4 lists the further proper-flat good-fiber loci. No unqualified same-S_k preservation is inferred.',
'EGAIV3,EGAIV4,Stacks056P','Proof: locally the quotient is coker(F --s→ F⊗L). The relative injectivity/flatness criterion gives openness; properness carries the closed bad locus to a closed parameter locus. A regular element lowers both depth and dimension by one.',group='relative_divisors')
add(14,'Seidenberg’s hypothesis',[25],1035,1052,'open on the pages','E',
'The author asks whether Seidenberg assumes normality or smoothness, and how his generic-section argument works.',
'Seidenberg1950 is explicitly about normal varieties. Samuel1951/1952 pp419–422 states normality, distinguishes it from nonsingularity, and explains irreducibility, codimension-one regularity, and the no-embedded-component step. Thus this bibliographic question is answered; the original AMS download was403, so the direct primary exposition is the checked witness.',
'Samuel1952,Seidenberg1950',group='seidenberg')
add(15,'All hyperplanes are connected under properness',[25,26],1053,1102,'proof sketched','B',
'Under the component-image and finite-image-deletion hypotheses of3.3, with X proper over an algebraically closed field, every hyperplane section is connected, including excess-dimensional sections.',
'For an integral source, EGA III1 Corollary4.3.7 and Remark4.3.8, printed133, gives the proper connectedness-specialization step over the normal dual projective space. Martinelli–Naranjo–Pirola Theorem1.1 supplies the irreducible projective case in any characteristic. The manuscript’s reducible case additionally uses its finite-image-deletion hypothesis; it is not a verbatim case of either cited theorem.',
'Stacks0G4C,EGAIII1,MartinelliNaranjoPirola2014','Proof: for an integral component apply Stein factorization to proper incidence. Its generic fiber is geometrically connected. The reduced finite factor is generically purely inseparable over the normal projective dual space; on a normal affine base each integral element has a p-power in the base, so this finite factor is universally injective and has connected fibers (birational in characteristic0). For reducible X, form the finite graph of components joined when their intersection has positive-dimensional image. The deletion hypothesis makes this graph connected. Such an intersection has proper positive-dimensional image, which meets every hyperplane. Each component section is connected, so their union is connected.',confidence='high',group='connectedness')
add(16,'Generic rational choices and property-improving sections',[27,28,29],1108,1224,'proof sketched','B',
'Over an infinite field a nonempty open in projective space has a rational point; it lifts locally for a projective bundle. Iterating good hyperplane sections of a projective flat fp family lowers the dimension of a specified bad locus. Differential/geometric-reducedness applications keep the unramified or characteristic0 restrictions.',
'This is standard Bertini and relative regular-section reasoning. EGAIV4§17.16 proves the related quasi-finite flat/étale quasi-section theorem; finite-field choices require finite separable extensions or a separate finite-field Bertini theorem.',
'EGAIV4,Stacks0FD4,Panin2025','Proof: a nonzero homogeneous polynomial cannot vanish at every rational point over an infinite field; choose coordinates on a projective-bundle chart and lift them over a base neighborhood. Each regular section drops the relevant positive dimension by one.',group='quasisections')
add(17,'Finite étale quasi-sections and the omitted projectivity',[30,31],1225,1293,'open on the pages','C',
'Proposition7.4 claims a finite étale local quasi-section for a proper flat family with the stated geometrically reduced locus; its proof invokes a projective hyperplane construction. The following7.5 warns that nonprojective proper smooth families can fail the finite assertion.',
'EGAIV4 17.16.1–3 proves quasi-finite flat/étale quasi-sections, not the claimed finite strengthening. The projective construction is supported under its explicit hypotheses. The exact nonprojective counterexample is not supplied or identified by the searches, so the literal proper-only statement is not certified.',
'EGAIV4,Stacks056P',confidence='medium',group='quasisections')
add(18,'Exceptional nonregular and excess-dimensional hyperplanes',[32,33],1298,1379,'false as stated','B',
'If every relevant component/associated-prime support has positive-dimensional image, the locus of hyperplanes containing such an image has codimension at least2. The transcription also asserts equality2 and suggests unrestricted preservation of S_k outside codimension2.',
'Equality is false: X=P^r has no excess-dimensional hyperplane sections. CM passes to a regular quotient, but S_k generally becomes S_(k−1); preserving S_k needs the extra depth criterion of8.5. Benoist2011 Th1.5 gives the exact depth-one obstruction for integral sections.',
'Benoist2011,EGAIV2','Proof: a fixed positive-dimensional projective image spans at least a line, so hyperplanes containing it have codimension≥2. For X=P^r the exceptional set is empty. At a closed depth-k point of dimension≥k+1, a regular hyperplane through it has depth k−1 and can fail S_k; the family through that point is a divisor.',
refine='The excess-dimension/nonregular-section locus has codimension≥2. Regular quotients preserve CM and satisfy S_(k−1) if the original module satisfies S_k; same-S_k preservation requires the separately specified closed-point depth condition.',group='exceptional_depth')
add(19,'Codimension drop for a closed subset',[34,35],1382,1441,'false as stated','B',
'Lemma8.4.1 as transcribed places codim≥k on Y⊂X, although its conclusion controls T∩Y. The surrounding proposition assumes codim(T,X)≥k and gives the usual drop by at most one, with contracted-component obstructions to a codimension-two exceptional bound.',
'The literal lemma is false. The principal ideal theorem gives the corrected local codimension statement; component and image bookkeeping yields the stated exceptional-locus description when the source’s local-dimension conventions are retained.',
'EGAIV2,Benoist2011','Proof: X=A², T=X, Y a line, k=1 satisfies the printed premise codim(Y,X)≥1. But T∩Y=Y has codimension0 in Y, so the purported positive bound fails. The correct premise codim(T,X)≥k excludes this example.',
refine='For a noetherian finite-dimensional scheme in the dimension-theoretic setting of the chapter, codim(T,X)≥k and an effective Cartier hyperplane imply codim(T∩H,X∩H)≥k−1; a codimension drop is analyzed componentwise with this premise on T.',group='exceptional_depth')
add(20,'Depth criterion for same-S_k sections',[35,36],1442,1567,'proof sketched','C',
'For quasi-finite f and coherent S_k module F with no contracted associated support, the draft’s codimension-two criterion requires depth F_x≥k+1 at every closed x with dim F_x≥k+1. In pure dimension d≥k+1 it is the stated bound codepth≤d−k−1.',
'The necessary obstruction follows directly by regular-section depth drop; Benoist2011 Th1.5 verifies the integral/S1 depth-one case sharply. EGAIV2 supplies the general local algebra. Searches did not locate a published statement matching every sheaf-valued componentwise iff in8.5, so that exact full criterion remains a proof-completion task here.',
'Benoist2011,EGAIV2','computations.py checks the integer dimension identities used later; proof_checks.md distinguishes the direct necessary argument from the full sufficiency not independently completed.',confidence='medium',group='exceptional_depth')
add(21,'Conormal description of singular hyperplanes',[37,38,39,40,41],1572,1726,'proof sketched','B',
'For smooth X/S and unramified f:X→P^r_S of constant relative dimension d<r, the scheme of singular hyperplane sections is P(N_X/P), where N is the conormal module in the quotient convention. Its projection to the dual space has supersingular locus equal to its ramification locus under the expected smooth-incidence hypotheses.',
'SGA7II XVII§3 and the separate SUP draft give the same conormal/differential geometry. The determinant formulation concerns polar nondegeneracy; SGA7’s ordinary quadratic singularity is broader in odd ambient rank in characteristic2.',
'SGA7II,Conrad2014','Proof: the universal section and first derivative vanish exactly when the hyperplane functional factors through the conormal kernel of f^*Omega_P/S→Omega_X/S. The Hessian is the derivative of the conormal projection, as checked in the complete-intersection diagram in proof_checks.md.',group='duality')
add(22,'Separable conormal projection and biduality',[42,43,44,45],1728,1851,'proof sketched','B',
'For smooth geometrically irreducible immersed X⊂P^r with nonempty conormal incidence, generic unramifiedness of the conormal projection is equivalent to a finite separable function-field extension and to a nonsupersingular tangency. Under these hypotheses the dual is a hypersurface and the conormal map is birational; over the smooth dual it is an open immersion, and an isomorphism if X is proper.',
'The exact separable-duality argument is developed in SGA7II XVII§3. Nonempty incidence and immersion are essential; neither a linear full-dimensional X nor arbitrary unramified multiple covers can be silently included in the birational statement.',
'SGA7II','Targeted proof check: the differential image at (x,H) identifies the hyperplane in the dual tangent space determined by x; two points over a smooth dual point must therefore coincide. Properness upgrades the open immersion to an isomorphism there.',group='duality')
add(23,'Characteristic0 pencils and the word ruled',[45,46,47,51],1852,1930,'corrected by himself later','B',
'In characteristic0, a nondefective smooth immersed variety has separable dual map, and a general pencil has at most one nondegenerate ordinary singularity in a bad fiber. The draft calls dual-defective varieties réglées, then explicitly rejects that terminology because smooth ruled quadric surfaces have a hypersurface dual.',
'SGA7II XVII2.5.2 supplies the characteristic0 Lefschetz-pencil result, and XVII§3 the duality proof. The terminology is an authorial withdrawal, not a modern theorem about classically ruled varieties.',
'SGA7II','computations.py quadric_tangent_section: x0x3−x1x2=0 has tangent section x1x2=0 at[1:0:0:0], while its dual is again a quadric hypersurface.',group='duality')
add(24,'Exceptional R_k, reducedness and normality criteria',[47,48,49,50],1931,2061,'proof sketched','C',
'The revised8.13 gives sufficient codimension-two conditions for preservation of geometric R_k using low-dimensional smooth dual-defective components.8.14 combines these with depth conditions for reducedness and normality.8.15 asks whether the initial R_k hypothesis is necessary, possibly with S1/equidimensionality.',
'The local R_k/S_k mechanism is standard in EGAIV2, and SGA7 controls ordinary singular sections. The manuscript deletes a necessity clause and retains doubt about another. Searches of the exact exceptional-locus formulations did not certify every iff; the residual is the componentwise necessity and stated generality, not generic Bertini normality.',
'EGAIV2,SGA7II,Benoist2011',confidence='medium',group='exceptional_depth')
add(25,'Normal integral sections outside codimension2',[50],2070,2085,'proof sketched','C',
'For proper geometrically integral normal X with depth≥3 at the relevant closed points and f unramified off codimension≥2, the page claims normal integral hyperplane sections outside a codimension≥2 exceptional locus.',
'Normality follows from the R1/S2 analysis provided its exact low-dimensional conditions hold; all-fiber connectedness then turns normality into integrality. Benoist2011 confirms the distinct integral bad-locus bounds. The unramified-off-codimension2 scope is not treated as proved merely by those results; its exceptional-image bookkeeping remains to be completed.',
'Benoist2011,SGA7II,EGAIV2',confidence='medium',group='exceptional_depth')
add(26,'The arbitrary-morphism irreducibility conjecture',[50,51],2086,2116,'false as stated','B',
'Conjecture8.17a predicts codimension≥2 for hyperplanes with reducible inverse image under an arbitrary morphism from geometrically irreducible X, provided dim f(X)≥3.',
'Poonen–Slavov Example1.7 gives the exact blow-up counterexample. Their Th1.5 restores the bound for constant-dimensional nonempty fibers; Th1.8 identifies the divisorial exceptions for arbitrary morphisms. First arXiv circulation23January2020, online4August2020, journal2022.',
'PoonenSlavov2022','computations.py blowup_total_transform: X=Bl_pP³ is smooth, proper, geometrically integral and f has3-dimensional image. Hyperplanes through p form a divisor in dualP³; their total transforms contain both the strict transform and exceptionalP². The chart x*(a+bu+cv) displays the two components.',
refine='For a geometrically irreducible variety X and f:X→P^r with all nonempty fibers of equal dimension, the bad geometric-irreducibility locus has dimension≤codim f(X)+1 (Poonen–Slavov Th1.5). In particular its codimension is≥2 when dim f(X)≥3.',group='poonenslavov')
add(27,'The surface obstruction and finite singular set criterion',[51,52],2117,2179,'proof sketched','C',
'A smooth quadric surface has a divisorial family of reducible tangent sections. Outside codimension2, an ordinary connected singular curve section has at most two geometric components.8.18 proposes an iff criterion for proper X with a finite exceptional set T using existence of irreducible sections through T and a singular irreducible section when the dual is nondefective.',
'The quadric example and two-branch node calculation are valid. Benoist2011 Th1.4 gives the sharp dimension-two bound. The full8.18 iff, including normalization and the dimension1/d−1 wording, is only sketched and was not matched exactly in the literature search.',
'Benoist2011,SGA7II','computations.py quadric_tangent_section verifies the surface example. A connected reduced curve with exactly one ordinary node and otherwise smooth has at most two components because the dual graph has at most one edge.',confidence='medium',group='surface_irreducibility')
add(28,'Fixed linear base and geometric irreducibility',[53,54],2186,2264,'false as stated','B',
'The fixed-linear-base version claims irreducibility from a projection image of dimension≥2 without explicitly excluding a fixed divisor on X.',
'Stacks0G4C Lemma37.32.3 explicitly includes the needed codimension≥2 base-locus condition. A fixed divisor is a direct obstruction even when the projection image is a surface.',
'Stacks0G4C','computations.py fixed_component_bertini: X=Segre(P¹×P²)⊂P⁵ is smooth geometrically integral; take base plane L0={u1=0}. Projection away from L0 has imageP², but every hyperplane containingL0 cuts u1*(a0v0+a1v1+a2v2)=0, a fixed divisor plus a residual divisor.',
refine='For a geometrically irreducible linear system with image dimension≥2, require that its base locus have codimension≥2 in X to conclude a general member is geometrically irreducible; use Stacks37.32.3 for the precise section-space formulation.',group='fixed_base')
add(29,'Veronese repair and its characteristic2 withdrawal',[55,159,160],2270,2305,'corrected by himself later','B',
'Proposition9.2 initially says every Veronese of degree n≥2 satisfies the separable-dual conditions8.8. Scans159–160 explicitly delete that assertion when char k=2 and dim X is odd, while retaining existence of ordinary quadratic tangencies.',
'SGA7II XVII2.5.1 proves the higher-Veronese Lefschetz existence theorem using ordinary quadratic singularities. The author’s own correction is essential: a smooth conic in characteristic2 has purely inseparable Gauss map of degree2.',
'SGA7II,Conrad2014','computations.py char2_conic_gradient gives [t²:0:s²] for the conic[s²:st:t²]. The extension k(s/t)/k((s/t)²) has degree2 and is inseparable. This satisfies smoothness and the degree2 Veronese hypothesis.',group='veronese')
add(30,'Fixed point and tangent-jet Veronese systems',[56,57],2306,2373,'proof sketched','B',
'The degree≥2/3 constructions use polynomial ratios to give image dimension≥2 for hyperplanes or hypersurfaces through a prescribed point or tangent jet. Geometric irreducibility also needs the inherited quasi-finite/immersion and codimension≥2 base-locus assumptions.',
'Stacks0FD4 Lemma33.47.1 gives the general high-degree fixed-subscheme immersion statement; Stacks0G4C gives the geometric-irreducibility criterion. The low degree calculations on the page are valid coordinate examples, not a theorem for arbitrary morphisms with fixed divisors.',
'Stacks0FD4,Stacks0G4C','Proof: for P=T1,Q=T1²,R=T1T2, the ratios Q/P=T1,R/P=T2 are algebraically independent. The cubic variant ratios Tr³/Tr²=Tr and Tr²T_(r−1)/Tr²=T_(r−1) work likewise. Squares in characteristic2 remain algebraically independent although the corresponding field extension can be inseparable.',group='fixed_base')
add(31,'Unfinished Lefschetz fundamental-group heading',[57],2370,2385,'open on the pages','F',
'The surviving9.4 is a heading;9.5 lists smooth/proper/geometrically integral hypotheses and a fundamental-group question without a completed conclusion.',
'SGA2 and SGA7 contain precise Lefschetz theorems, but no unique exact assertion can be recovered from this heading. It is not evidence that a particular fundamental-group theorem was proved or remains open.',
'SGA7II',confidence='low',group='lefschetz_heading')
add(32,'Pencils, axes and blow-ups',[58,59,60,61],2386,2528,'proof sketched','B',
'For a regular rank2 section defining an axis T, the incidence pencil is Bl_T X. A generic pencil avoids a closed bad locus of codimension≥2. The deleted10.3 suggests the higher-rank regular-section/Rees-algebra version but leaves its proof to be supplied.',
'The regular-ideal Rees construction is standard; Stacks01OF gives the blow-up universal property. SGA7II XVII2 defines the pencil blow-up and its ordinary singular fibers. A single line avoiding a nonclosed constructible subset does not prove the codimension bound for its closure.',
'Stacks01OF,SGA7II','Proof: in a regular two-generator ideal(a,b), its Rees algebra is A[U,V]/(bU−aV), giving exactly the pencil incidence. Basis changes preserve the Koszul regularity in the local regular-immersion setting. The counterexample to replacing a closed bad locus by an arbitrary constructible one is a line with its intersection point with the chosen pencil-line removed.',group='pencils')
add(33,'Curve pencils',[61],2529,2557,'proof sketched','B',
'For a smooth geometrically connected projective curve over an infinite field, a sufficiently ample degree≥2 Veronese pencil gives a finite generically étale morphism toP¹ whose geometric fibers are reduced or contain one length2 ordinary double point and otherwise reduced points.',
'This is the curve case of SGA7II XVII2.5.1. In characteristic2 the length2 exceptional fiber can be wild; it does not imply separability of the conormal dual map. The bad point is rational over the parameter’s residue field, not necessarily over the original k.',
'SGA7II','computations.py conic_pencil_branch_values gives the characteristic0 degree2 example with two distinct branch values. This finite example does not establish the all-curves theorem.',group='pencils')
